      q: 'Sahlins maps kinds of reciprocity onto social distance. Giving without expectation of return (parent to child) versus balanced exchange (trade between strangers) illustrates what principle?',
      choices: ['Only strangers trade', 'The mode of exchange encodes the relationship — exchange systems are social maps', 'Kinship is economically inefficient', 'Markets came first historically'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Generalised reciprocity near, negative reciprocity far: what you may expect back encodes who someone is to you. Exchange is not just moving goods — it is continuously making and marking social relations.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Polanyi called pre-modern economies "embedded". What does that mean?',
      choices: ['They were hidden', 'Economic activity was subordinate to and organised by social relations — kinship, ritual, reciprocity — rather than by markets', 'They had no production', 'They used no money'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Distribution ran through institutions of relationship and obligation; the market as an autonomous, self-regulating sphere is a recent, deliberately constructed design — not humanity\'s default economy.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Among the Tiv, Bohannan found exchanges were sorted into ranked "spheres" (food, marriage goods, prestige items), with conversion between spheres morally fraught. What does this show about money?',
      choices: ['The Tiv lacked money entirely', 'Universal-purpose money is one cultural design; many societies run multiple morally bounded value spheres', 'Spheres are the same as markets', 'The Tiv rejected trade'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Conveyance within a sphere is ordinary exchange; conversion (food for wives, say) threatens the moral order. Colonial currency then collapsed the spheres — a worked example of how one money redesign rewired a moral economy.',
    },
    {
      q: 'The Nuer (Evans-Pritchard) had no chiefs, yet feud was limited by segmentary opposition: "I against my brother; my brother and I against my cousin..." What does this show?',
      choices: ['They were constantly at war', 'Political order can be produced by social structure itself — alignment against common opponents without a central authority', 'They had a secret king', 'Feud cannot be regulated'],
      answer: 1,
      why: 'Nested segments fused against larger threats and separated otherwise — ordered anarchy. Constraint on violence came from the geometry of kinship and the balance of oppositions, not from a state.',
    },
  ],
};

